WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:02.168
-Welcome back to "Late Night"!

00:00:02.168 --> 00:00:03.502
We're here with Sutton Foster
and Hugh Jackman.

00:00:03.502 --> 00:00:05.939
And it is so lovely
to have you guys.

00:00:05.939 --> 00:00:07.206
And, Hugh,
I want to ask about this.

00:00:07.206 --> 00:00:08.241
You were talking about,
you know,

00:00:08.241 --> 00:00:10.043
early days of being an actor.

00:00:10.043 --> 00:00:12.545
You gave a speech
that sort of went viral

00:00:12.545 --> 00:00:15.382
after one of your shows,
talking about understudies

00:00:15.382 --> 00:00:19.252
and how, I think, none of us
quite appreciate the pressure

00:00:19.252 --> 00:00:21.220
they're under
when they have to go out,

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:23.589
especially for a show
of this magnitude,

00:00:23.589 --> 00:00:24.924
and do a part.

00:00:24.924 --> 00:00:26.292
-Yeah, I found it really moving.

00:00:26.292 --> 00:00:28.394
I think a lot of people
understand understudy

00:00:28.394 --> 00:00:29.763
that's been
in a bunch of movies.

00:00:29.763 --> 00:00:31.497
But there's a thing
called a swing,

00:00:31.497 --> 00:00:33.033
which I don't think
anyone understood.

00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:35.668
So, Kathy Voytko,
who was the understudy

00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:38.905
and a swing for Sutton,
who got COVID --

00:00:38.905 --> 00:00:40.172
Am I allowed to say that?
-Yeah.

00:00:40.172 --> 00:00:43.075
-Yeah, she got COVID.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:43.075 --> 00:00:44.810
We all got COVID.
-No, she said that she was --

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:46.045
That's what
she wants you to say.

00:00:46.045 --> 00:00:48.681
-"COVID."
[ Laughter ]

00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:50.583
But it was like,
our fifth or sixth preview,

00:00:50.583 --> 00:00:53.886
so no one had any rehearsal.
It was a crazy time.

00:00:53.886 --> 00:00:55.721
Like, no one -- there's
no understudy rehearsals.

00:00:55.721 --> 00:00:58.658
But a swing covers
eight or nine parts,

00:00:58.658 --> 00:01:00.259
so a swing is not on stage
every night.

00:01:00.259 --> 00:01:02.228
They're just, like,
ready to take over.

00:01:02.228 --> 00:01:04.130
-Yeah.
-And so she arrived --

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:05.598
Kathy arrived at 12:00.

00:01:05.598 --> 00:01:07.400
And we said, "Oh, by the way,
you're on tonight."

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.868
She goes,
"Oh, okay, which track?"

00:01:08.868 --> 00:01:10.803
And I said, "Uh, Marian Paroo."

00:01:10.803 --> 00:01:13.439
She went, "The lead.
Right, okay."

00:01:13.439 --> 00:01:14.974
And they handed her
an abbreviated version

00:01:14.974 --> 00:01:16.242
of the script.

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:19.044
Like, a little file with
all her scenes highlighted.

00:01:19.044 --> 00:01:21.614
And said, "Don't worry, we have
like six or seven hours

00:01:21.614 --> 00:01:23.515
before the show, so we're gonna
go through everything."

00:01:23.515 --> 00:01:25.985
And I just saw her face
just go completely white.

00:01:25.985 --> 00:01:29.155
And I was just blown away.
I mean, Sutton, quite famously,

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:30.923
became a star
being an understudy.

00:01:30.923 --> 00:01:32.825
And I'd never done it,
so, for me,

00:01:32.825 --> 00:01:36.395
it is so brave
and incredible and Herculean.

00:01:36.395 --> 00:01:39.532
And Kathy went on with,
basically, running through

00:01:39.532 --> 00:01:42.001
every scene and every song once,

00:01:42.001 --> 00:01:43.937
and then went on
in front of 1,500 people.

00:01:43.937 --> 00:01:46.573
And I just wanted the audience
to kind of understand that.

00:01:46.573 --> 00:01:50.509
-It's a really lovely gift you
gave the audience, as well.

00:01:50.509 --> 00:01:52.945
I mean, not just a gift for her,
but I think sometimes

00:01:52.945 --> 00:01:54.848
an audience member might have
a moment of like,

00:01:54.848 --> 00:01:56.582
"Oh, there's
an understudy tonight."

00:01:56.582 --> 00:01:58.951
When you see that,
you should realize you're about

00:01:58.951 --> 00:02:02.355
to see somebody do something
that requires so much skill.

00:02:02.355 --> 00:02:04.057
-Yeah.
-And that's a really exciting

00:02:04.057 --> 00:02:05.458
thing to be for
on any given night.

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:07.093
-And audiences are
actually really --

00:02:07.093 --> 00:02:08.294
Sorry, I'm talking too much.

00:02:08.294 --> 00:02:11.164
But audiences are really
generous with that.

00:02:11.164 --> 00:02:14.567
Although this was the fifth in a
preview for a show like this.

00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:16.602
The people who are going
had bought their tickets

00:02:16.602 --> 00:02:20.072
a long way before, and Sutton is
literally the best in the world.

00:02:20.072 --> 00:02:21.307
And I would say this
if you weren't here

00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:22.775
or if I wasn't
on national television.

00:02:22.775 --> 00:02:26.011
But you are, I think, the best
in the world at what she does.

00:02:26.011 --> 00:02:28.081
She's incredible.
So people are going to see her.

00:02:28.081 --> 00:02:32.651
So I can only imagine they're
like, "Aww!" when she wasn't on.

00:02:32.651 --> 00:02:34.120
But they sort of rally.
-Yeah.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:36.656
-And they understand the story
of it, which is amazing.

00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:39.024
-And anything that's unique
to the night, as you said,

00:02:39.024 --> 00:02:40.125
like that's one of
the cool things

00:02:40.125 --> 00:02:41.360
about going to see
a Broadway show.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:43.429
Like, it was different.
It wasn't the same.

00:02:43.429 --> 00:02:44.697
And probably
wasn't they were expecting.

00:02:44.697 --> 00:02:46.766
-It was spontaneous and alive,
yeah.

00:02:46.766 --> 00:02:49.335
-Talk about your experience
as an understudy.

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:51.571
What happened on the nights
you were told to go out there?

00:02:51.571 --> 00:02:53.973
-Oh, gosh.
Well, yes, that was --

00:02:53.973 --> 00:02:56.709
I mean, my early days,
I was in the ensemble

00:02:56.709 --> 00:03:00.212
and was an understudy.
And it was always a thrill.

00:03:00.212 --> 00:03:04.917
But you had to be prepared and
ready for that phone call.

00:03:04.917 --> 00:03:07.086
But it was always scary,
and you just try to keep

00:03:07.086 --> 00:03:10.356
the show up and try not
to throw anybody too much.

00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:14.661
But our company -- ooh, sorry.
Our company is --

00:03:14.661 --> 00:03:16.662
[ Laughter ]

00:03:16.662 --> 00:03:21.234
Our company is extraordinary,
and what everyone did,

00:03:21.234 --> 00:03:23.402
especially when we were
first opening

00:03:23.402 --> 00:03:26.239
and how everyone
rose to the occasion,

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:29.141
it was like an unbelievable,

00:03:29.141 --> 00:03:33.145
unbelievable talent
collaboration.

00:03:33.145 --> 00:03:35.882
Everybody just kind of came
together to keep the show going.

00:03:35.882 --> 00:03:38.885
At one point, there were 12
people out of our --

00:03:38.885 --> 00:03:41.987
-14.
-14 people out of our show.

00:03:41.987 --> 00:03:44.891
And swings, Ryan Worsing
was doing seven tracks.

00:03:44.891 --> 00:03:46.725
-Yeah.
-And they were just trying

00:03:46.725 --> 00:03:48.894
to keep the show up.

00:03:48.894 --> 00:03:50.496
-Does anyone know the script?
Because just come on down.

00:03:50.496 --> 00:03:52.698
[ Laughter ]

00:03:52.698 --> 00:03:55.601
-Dance Captain Ryan,
who's incredible,

00:03:55.601 --> 00:03:57.503
has done 15
different tracks already.

00:03:57.503 --> 00:03:58.704
-Yeah.
-So he's played

00:03:58.704 --> 00:04:00.573
15 different parts.
-And he's just awesome.

00:04:00.573 --> 00:04:02.908
And he's amazing,
and everybody just --

00:04:02.908 --> 00:04:04.377
everyone rallies
around each other,

00:04:04.377 --> 00:04:05.678
and they support one another.

00:04:05.678 --> 00:04:08.047
It's the most incredible company
I've ever been a part of.

00:04:08.047 --> 00:04:10.783
And it's really extraordinary.
-It's mindblowing when I --

00:04:10.783 --> 00:04:13.085
Any time I see a,
be it a musical or a play

00:04:13.085 --> 00:04:15.488
on Broadway, I can't believe
someone's learned one part.

00:04:15.488 --> 00:04:17.256
-Right. Let alone 15.
-And the fact you have to learn

00:04:17.256 --> 00:04:19.926
all the parts is just amazing.
-Yeah, and be confident

00:04:19.926 --> 00:04:21.760
and, like, ready to go
and ready to sing

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:23.629
whatever you're supposed
to sing, and, yeah.

00:04:23.629 --> 00:04:25.932
It's amazing.
-Well, it shows.

00:04:25.932 --> 00:04:28.067
What you're saying about
the company shows on stage.

00:04:28.067 --> 00:04:29.902
Congratulations, guys.
It's such a wonderful show.

00:04:29.902 --> 00:04:31.337
I hope everybody
has a chance to see it.

00:04:31.337 --> 00:04:32.638
And thanks so much
for being here.

00:04:32.638 --> 00:04:35.341
-It's always a pleasure.
-Sutton Foster, Hugh Jackman.

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:37.209
"The Music Man," playing at

00:04:37.209 --> 00:04:38.577
the Winter Garden Theatre
on Broadway.

00:04:38.577 --> 00:04:40.246
We'll be right back
with Ali Wentworth.

